  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe coldest morning of the season is upon us. Temperatures have \ndipped below 30F here at the office at the time of writing with the \nexpectation that temperatures will continue to fall into the low to \nmid 20s across the region. It would not be surprising to see a few \nspots dip into the upper teens across central MO. The primary \ncatalyst is a stout ridge visible across all height levels. At low \nlevels, the center of this ridge envelopes areas from northern MO to \nwestern Ontario. Aloft, northwest flow pushes colder Canadian air \nsouthward. The positioning of this lower level high with antecedent \nlight winds creates an environment for strong nocturnal cooling. \nHowever, model sounding analyses combined with upper level water \nvapor imagery shows a channel of moisture stretching from eastern MT \nsoutheastward toward NW AR. At the upper levels, this extends into \neastern KS and is slowly migrating toward western MO. These clouds, \nthough nearly 30-40k feet above the surface, may provide some cover \nslightly reducing the rate of nocturnal cooling to keep a vast \nmajority of the region at or above 20F. This is boosted by patches \nof low level saturation as temperatures decrease at a slightly \nfaster rate than dew points through the night. As the sun rises, low \nlevel winds shift back southerly as the overall pattern transients \neastward. While winds are expected to be light through the day (5-10 \nMPH), this southerly shift will be enough to bump highs up by 2-4 \ndegrees compared to yesterday with most of the region topping out in \nthe upper 40s to near 50. The trend of southerly warm air advection \nlooks to continue through next week likely swinging temperatures \nback to well above normal.\n\nAttention turns west through the weekend as a large scale trough \nattempts moves onshore. The hemispheric wave that aggregates the \nperturbations stretches from the Pacific coast to the Aleutians. \nThe center of which looks to remain stalled in the Gulf of Alaska. \nIt is expected for the 500mb trough axis centered near the Four \nCorners region to eject and spiral along the Red River valley of \nOK/TX. This briefly redirects the flow of warm air; stalling the \nwarming trend Monday. Southerly flow quickly reforms Tuesday lifting \nhighs to the 60s by mid next week with upper 60s to some 70s \nexpected late next week.\n\nDry weather also dominates the next 5 days before precipitation \nchances return. The Aleutian wave mentioned earlier finally \nprogresses eastward and moves into the Pacific northwest. As it does \nso, a ridge across the eastern CONUS facilitates a tightening \npressure gradient across the central CONUS. This funnels Gulf \nmoisture and warm air northward. Several shortwaves ejecting ahead \nof the main trough catalyze the development of showers across the \ncentral part of the country. Guidance remains varied about where \nstorms will track. A glancing chance of rain, mainly for NW MO on \nThursday, followed by more geographically spread, yet still \nrelatively low (10-30 percent) through Friday morning. As next \nweekend approaches accelerated midlevel flow, upper level CVA, and \nsome surface kinematic and thermal dynamics looks to bring the best \nchances for showers (and some embedded thunderstorms) next Saturday \n(40-60 percent). It is still far too early to speculate the nitty- \ngritty details of next weekend's forecast as the macroscale movement \nof the Pacific wave and the synoptic evolution beneath it through \nthe weekend will greatly affect the anticipated weather pattern over \nthe next 7-9 days.",
